# Shared review rooms

## Architecture

- `src/teams.ts` – Hono sub-app mounted at `/api/teams`. Every route authenticates first (401), then requires a
  same-origin `Origin` for mutations (403). Membership is always read from D1 (`team_members`); nothing is cached.
  Non-members get 403.
- Roles: `owner` (immutable, exactly one), `member` (read/write) and `viewer` (read-only). Viewers can list teams,
  connect to rooms, receive history/live messages and download files; uploading, deleting files, sending messages and
  membership management return 403 (HTTP) or an `error` event with `code:"forbidden"` (socket). `GET /api/teams`
  returns the actual role.
- Migrations (append-only, earlier files untouched): `0002_teams.sql`, `0003_team_uploads.sql`, and
  `0004_team_viewer_role.sql`, which rebuilds `team_members` (SQLite cannot alter a CHECK) to allow `viewer` and adds
  `grant_id`. Existing rows are copied with fresh grant ids. A grant id is minted when a membership is created and
  survives role changes; removal deletes the row, so re-adding yields a new grant.
- Team uploads: R2 key is `teams/<team>/uploads/<server uuid>`, rebuilt from the verified team and a D1 row looked up by
  `(id, team_id)`; a foreign ID returns 404 on GET/DELETE and no bytes are touched. Bodies are bounded while streaming
  (`readBounded`, also covers chunked input). Personal `/api/uploads` is unchanged and separate.
- `src/room.ts` – `TeamRoom`, a SQLite Durable Object, one per `<team>:<room>` (no new DO migration tag; the schema
  is upgraded in the constructor). Hibernation API (`acceptWebSocket`, `serializeAttachment`, `webSocketMessage/Close/Error`)
  and `setWebSocketAutoResponse` for `ping`/`pong`. Messages are inserted into SQLite (`seq INTEGER PRIMARY KEY
  AUTOINCREMENT`) before being broadcast, so `seq` stays strictly increasing across restarts and pruning (latest 1000
  rows kept; snapshots send 50). Old rooms gain a nullable `client_id` column via `ALTER TABLE`; legacy rows stay readable.
- The Worker's WebSocket route validates the room name, rejects query strings, requires same-origin `Origin`, checks
  membership, and forwards a freshly built request containing only upgrade headers plus `X-Verified-*` headers
  derived from the session. Client-supplied identity headers are never forwarded. The DO is only reachable via the
  binding and exposes no other entry point; there is no public fanout/control route.
- Live authorization: the lease from the first release is gone. Each application send is handled in a serial queue and
  (1) re-checks the sender's session (not revoked/expired) and membership grant in D1, closing the socket (1008) if gone
  and rejecting viewers, *before* any dedup lookup, conflict, ack or commit; (2) commits; (3) re-checks every
  recipient before delivering that message, closing and skipping revoked ones. So removal, downgrade and logout apply to
  open sockets from the moment the HTTP response completes. A socket opened under a removed membership stays dead if
  the user is re-added (new grant id); they must reconnect. Logging out is never undone by re-adding.
- Retry safety: `{text, clientId}` with `clientId` matching `[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,80}` (invalid -> `error` code `invalid`).
  The room table `client_ids (user_id, client_id) -> seq, text` is written in the same SQLite transaction as the message
  and is never pruned, so it survives the history window and restarts. An exact retry gets `{type:"ack",seq,clientId}`
  on the retrying socket only; different text gets `{type:"error",code:"conflict",clientId}`. The serial queue makes
  simultaneous retries on two sockets commit exactly once. Message events and snapshots include `clientId` when present.
- `public/teams.html` – UI with the required `data-testid` controls; text rendered with `textContent`; reconnect closes
  the previous socket first (generation counter ignores stale sockets); seqs already rendered are skipped. For a
  viewer, `message-text`/`message-send`/`team-upload` are disabled. The UI sends a fresh `clientId` with each message
  and never resends automatically.

## Commands

`pnpm typecheck`, `pnpm test`, `pnpm build`, `pnpm db:local` (or the equivalent project tools). Tests:
`test/teams.test.ts` (phase one), `test/roles.test.ts` (viewer role, open-socket revocation, retry/dedup).

## Limitations

- The five-minute lease no longer applies; instead each send costs a few D1 reads (sender + each recipient).
- Revoked idle sockets are only closed when the next message in that room is sent; they receive nothing in the meantime.
- Deduplication records are kept forever (one small row per distinct `clientId`) and store the original text.
- No exactly-once delivery: a client that disconnects mid-send gets no replay beyond the 50-message snapshot; clients
  must retry with the same `clientId`.
- The UI only learns a changed role after refresh (server enforcement is immediate). The team file list shows only
  files uploaded in the current page session (no list endpoint). Bytes of removed members' files are never
  garbage-collected; there is no team deletion. Membership management is API-only.
- Restart persistence of room history relies on Durable Object SQLite storage and is not covered by an automated test
  (Wrangler persists local state by default).
